 ### Sparse Neural Voxel Grid (SNeRG)
 A model storing a precomputed NeRF on a sparse voxel grid, sometimes referred to as 'baking' [S5]. During evaluation, an MLP produces specular color, which is combined with alpha compositing of the specular color along the ray to yield final pixel color [S5]. The layout stores precomputed diffuse color, density, and feature vectors in a sparse voxel grid [S5]. It is approximately 3000 times faster than the original NeRF implementation [S5].
 
+### Attention-based Color Mapping
+Disentangles color distribution from object identity (pseudo-semantics) using an attention mechanism to lookup semantic embeddings, allowing color manipulation and equivariance to color transformations [S6]. Model a latent radiance field of keys K, where each key represents a semantic embedding at a specific spatial position [S6]. Multiply keys K with a fixed set of queries Q (learned prototype embeddings for each semantic channel) [S6]. Process the result through a softmax to produce a probability distribution across semantic channels [S6]. Multiply the probability distribution with values V (color for each semantic channel) produced by a color network to get final RGB colors [S6]. During training, use the same latent code for both the key/query generation and the color network to learn the correct data distribution [S6]. At inference, split the latent code to swap the color distribution among different object identities or randomize it entirely [S6]. The color mapping operator is linear w.r.t. colors, allowing application before (in radiance field sampler) or after (in rendered semantic image) rendering, unlike non-linear texture lookups [S6].
+
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Faster than pure MLP-based NeRF due to the shallow MLP and grid lookups. Training converges faster than grid-only methods [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Depends on grid dimension $D$. Larger $D$ increases memory usage but improves quality [S1]. Free-space skipping strategies can reduce memory consumption [S1].
-*   **Measured Performance:** Baseline ($D=12$, 2 layers, 128 ch) achieves PSNR 35.54 in 7.3 minutes. High-res ($D=24$, 2 layers, 128 ch) achieves PSNR 35.90 in 8.8 minutes [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Faster than pure MLP-based NeRF due to the shallow MLP and grid lookups [S1]. Training converges faster than grid-only methods [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Depends on grid dimension $D$ [S1]. Larger $D$ increases memory usage but improves quality [S1]. Free-space skipping strategies can reduce memory consumption [S1].
